title: Veeam: Backup Jobs via REST API
agents: special
catalog: os/storage
license: GPLv2
distribution: check_mk
description:
 This check monitors the status of backup jobs reported by the Veeam
 Backup & Replication REST API, collected via the Veeam special agent.
 It reports the same information as the {VEEAM Job} service from the
 Veeam agent plug-in, and additionally reports the time since the last
 run, the next scheduled run (except for a disabled job), the target
 repository and the number of objects processed.

 The summary always shows three fields:

 Status: the job status as Veeam reports it: Running, Inactive,
 Disabled, Enabled, Stopping, Stopped or Starting. A disabled job always
 shows "Disabled", also where Veeam reports it as Stopped.

 Result: the result of the last run: Success, Warning or Failed. While
 a run is in progress, Veeam reports no result, which is shown as
 "In progress". A job that has never run shows "Not run".

 Time since last run: the time since the last run started. While a run
 is in progress, this is the time since the current run started. It is
 left out for a job that has never run.

 This check is {OK} if the result of the last run is Success. It is
 {WARN} if the result is Warning. It is {CRIT} if the result is Failed.
 A job with no result yet, either because it has never run or because a
 run is currently in progress, is {OK}. A disabled job is {OK} by default;
 the state is configurable in the rule "Veeam: Backup job". Its last result
 is shown for information only.

 Levels can be set for the time since the last run in the rule "Veeam:
 Backup job"; there are none by default. They are not applied to disabled
 jobs or to jobs that have never run.

 A job that disappears from the API output makes the service {UNKNOWN}.

discovery:
 One service is created for each backup job reported by the API, including
 disabled ones. To leave out disabled jobs, disable this in the rule "Veeam:
 Backup job discovery".

item:
 The name of the backup job, sanitized: spaces and apostrophes are replaced
 by underscores, followed by the first 8 characters of the job's Veeam ID
 in parentheses, e.g. "Daily_VMs (1a2b3c4d)".
